---
import ArticleLayout from "@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "How to Convert an Array to JSON in JavaScript (JSON.stringify)";
const description =
  "Convert any JavaScript array to a JSON string with JSON.stringify() — arrays of objects, pretty-printing with indentation, common gotchas (null items, dates, circular refs), and parsing back.";
const keywords =
  "array to json in javascript, convert array to json javascript, javascript array to json string, json.stringify array, js array to json";

const s1 = `const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"];
const json = JSON.stringify(fruits);

console.log(json);
// '["apple","banana","cherry"]'  (a string)`;

const s2 = `const users = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada" },
  { id: 2, name: "Linus" },
];

JSON.stringify(users);
// '[{"id":1,"name":"Ada"},{"id":2,"name":"Linus"}]'`;

const s3 = `JSON.stringify(users, null, 2);
// [
//   {
//     "id": 1,
//     "name": "Ada"
//   },
//   ...
// ]`;

const s4 = `JSON.stringify([undefined, function () {}, Symbol("x")]);
// '[null,null,null]'   -> non-serialisable items become null

JSON.stringify([new Date(0)]);
// '["1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"]'   -> Date becomes an ISO string

JSON.stringify([NaN, Infinity]);
// '[null,null]'

const a = {};
a.self = a;
JSON.stringify([a]);
// TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON`;

const s5 = `const json = '["apple","banana"]';
const arr = JSON.parse(json);

Array.isArray(arr); // true
arr[0];             // "apple"`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is the difference between a JavaScript array and a JSON array?",
    a: "A JavaScript array is a live, in-memory object you can push to, map over, and mutate. A JSON array is text — a string that follows the JSON grammar, used to store or transmit that data. JSON.stringify() turns the live array into the text form; JSON.parse() turns the text back into a live array. They look similar because JSON's array syntax was based on JavaScript's, but one is data in memory and the other is a string.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why did some of my array items become null in the JSON?",
    a: "JSON has no way to represent undefined, functions, or symbols. When one of those appears as an array element, JSON.stringify() replaces it with null so the array positions stay aligned. (Inside an object, such properties are dropped entirely instead.) NaN and Infinity also become null. If you see unexpected nulls, one of these non-serialisable values is the cause.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I turn the array into a downloadable .json file?",
    a: "Pretty-print it with JSON.stringify(arr, null, 2), wrap the string in a Blob of type application/json, create an object URL with URL.createObjectURL(blob), and click a temporary anchor with a download attribute. Or skip the code: paste the array output into JSON Formatter Pro and use its Download button — everything stays in your browser.",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  keywords={keywords}
  path="/learn/array-to-json-javascript"
  heading="How to Convert an Array to JSON in JavaScript"
  dek="Use JSON.stringify() to turn any array — including arrays of objects — into a JSON string, with formatting options and the edge cases to watch for."
  datePublished="2026-08-09"
  dateModified="2026-08-09"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="answer">
    To convert an array to JSON in JavaScript, pass it to <code>JSON.stringify()</code>. It accepts
    any array — of strings, numbers, booleans, objects, or nested arrays — and returns a
    JSON-formatted <strong>string</strong>. For example, <code>JSON.stringify(["a", "b"])</code>
    returns <code>'["a","b"]'</code>. Add a third argument, <code>JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)</code>,
    to pretty-print it with two-space indentation.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{s1}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The result is a <strong>string</strong>, not an array — that is the whole point. You now have a
    portable text form you can send in a request body, write to a file, or store in a database
    column, and later restore with <code>JSON.parse()</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Convert an array of objects to JSON</h2>
  <p>
    The same call works for an array of objects — the most common real-world case, such as rows from
    an API or a database query. Every object is serialised in order:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s2}</code></pre>

  <h2>Pretty-print the JSON output</h2>
  <p>
    <code>JSON.stringify()</code> takes two optional arguments: a <em>replacer</em> and a
    <em>space</em> value. Pass <code>null</code> for the replacer and a number (or a string like a
    tab) for the space to get readable, indented output:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s3}</code></pre>
  <p>
    That indentation is exactly what a formatter does. If you would rather paste the array's output
    and beautify or validate it visually, the
    <a href="/json-formatter/">JSON formatter</a> and
    <a href="/json-editor/">JSON editor</a> do it in your browser, with nothing uploaded.
  </p>

  <h2>Common gotchas when stringifying arrays</h2>
  <p>
    JSON is a smaller data model than JavaScript, so a few values do not survive the round trip.
    These are the ones that surprise people:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s4}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>undefined, functions, symbols</strong> — become <code>null</code> inside an array (and are dropped inside an object).</li>
    <li><strong>Date</strong> — serialised to an ISO 8601 string via its <code>toJSON()</code> method.</li>
    <li><strong>NaN and Infinity</strong> — become <code>null</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>BigInt</strong> — throws a <code>TypeError</code>; convert it to a string or number first.</li>
    <li><strong>Circular references</strong> — throw a <code>TypeError</code>; break the cycle before stringifying.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Turn the JSON string back into an array</h2>
  <p>
    The inverse of <code>JSON.stringify()</code> is <code>JSON.parse()</code>, which reads the text
    and returns a live array you can work with again:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s5}</code></pre>

  <div slot="related">
    Working with a real payload? Paste it into the
    <a href="/json-formatter/" class="text-accent hover:underline">JSON formatter</a> to beautify and
    validate it, or the <a href="/json-editor/" class="text-accent hover:underline">JSON editor</a> to
    fix it with live error highlighting — all client-side, nothing uploaded.
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
